WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.850
Pilar, take it away.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:04.880
Teach us how to cook pasta.

3
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.250
Amatriciana.

4
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.560
Out of the essentials of Italian cooking

5
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.630
by Marcella Hazan on page 157.

6
00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.730
We want you to cook with us.

7
00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:17.880
So you at home who are kind enough to be watching us

8
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.020
please submit photos, videos, anything you got

9
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.440
to join us in this culinary venture.

10
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.273
All right.

11
00:00:25.273 --> 00:00:26.106
Lynn, Daniel and Zenobia

12
00:00:26.106 --> 00:00:28.270
feel free to ask questions while we go.

13
00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:29.200
Pilar, take it away.

14
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.541
All right.

15
00:00:30.541 --> 00:00:31.890 line:15%
So we're doing them Amatriciana sauce.

16
00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.560 line:15%
And one of the reasons why I love this pasta is

17
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.309 line:15%
because it's super simple and it has pancetta in it.

18
00:00:39.309 --> 00:00:42.208 line:15%
And it uses bucatini pasta

19
00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:44.380 line:15%
which is one of my favorite, favorite pasta shapes.

20
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.930 line:15%
It's like a spaghetti sexy cuts in, you know

21
00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.060 line:15%
it's like bouncing.

22
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:48.893 line:15%
I like it.

23
00:00:48.893 --> 00:00:50.550 line:15%
All right, so first we're going to do,

24
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.120
you're going to add two tablespoons of oil.

25
00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.130
Right there.

26
00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:55.430
Yup, looks so clear.

27
00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.052 line:15%
Yup, it is a vegetable oil.

28
00:00:57.052 --> 00:01:00.723 line:15%
Ah, and we're going to do one tablespoon of butter.

29
00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:07.030 line:15%
And over here we have one medium onion.

30
00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:10.130
Does it matter what type of onion you use,

31
00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.283
could it be like red?

32
00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:15.840
So I think that for this recipe, they use a yellow

33
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.771
which is kind of the standard workhorse onion.

34
00:01:18.771 --> 00:01:22.720
Vidalias could be used but those are a little sweeter.

35
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:23.810
White onions as well.

36
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.550
But I am actually a shallot fiend.

37
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.560
And if you were looking to substitute

38
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.580
a different kind of onion,

39
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:32.663
I feel like give shallots a go for sure.

40
00:01:32.663 --> 00:01:33.662
I love them.

41
00:01:33.662 --> 00:01:36.450
So the onions now are getting sort of pale and golden

42
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:40.453
and now we're going to add in our pancetta.

43
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:47.550
So basically pancetta is uncured pork belly.

44
00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:48.790
And we're just going to saute that

45
00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:52.490
for about a minute to let the flavors meld with the onion.

46
00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.860
Zenobia, you had a question for Pilar?

47
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.025
Yes, I do.

48
00:01:57.025 --> 00:02:01.590
Can the pancetta be substituted for regular bacon?

49
00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.490
Will it take away from the recipe?

50
00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.440
That's a really good question.

51
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.090
So things like guanciale and pancetta are actually

52
00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.180
cured pork products where they're not smoked,

53
00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.310
whereas bacon is a smoked product.

54
00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.840 line:15%
So just be mindful that when you're using that,

55
00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.230 line:15%
the bacon's actually going to be less salty

56
00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.010 line:15%
but it's going to give it that smoky flavor.

57
00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:22.900
And now we're going to add

58
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:27.900
in our one and a half cups of chopped canned tomatoes.

59
00:02:31.477 --> 00:02:33.317
And we're going to give that a good simmer.

60
00:02:33.317 --> 00:02:34.150 line:15%
I have a question about the sauce.

61
00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:35.580 line:15%
Yes?

62
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.250
So when you add the tomato sauce,

63
00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.470
sometimes I find that I simmer it too far down.

64
00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.830
Is it okay to add some more of that tomato sauce?

65
00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:44.900
Absolutely.

66
00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.960
So what I like to do when you drain off a can of tomatoes

67
00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:51.450
is actually reserve the liquid until you're finished cooking

68
00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.430
because sometimes it might reduce too much

69
00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.720
and you can always add more later.

70
00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.780
So definitely keep the sauce until you're done cooking.

71
00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.133
So you make sure all your bases are covered.

72
00:03:02.103 --> 00:03:03.970
Great, thank you.

73
00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:05.286
Can you use fresh tomatoes?

74
00:03:05.286 --> 00:03:09.190
So you can, they're gonna take a little longer

75
00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:10.330
to cook down.

76
00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:12.150
And what I would recommend is

77
00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.760
if you're going to use fresh tomatoes

78
00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.532
really use them at the height of their season.

79
00:03:16.532 --> 00:03:19.330
That way they're going to be extra, extra delicious.

80
00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:22.150
And do you want seed or deseed?

81
00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:24.158 line:15%
I don't mind the seed.

82
00:03:24.158 --> 00:03:26.899 line:15%
I feel like deseeding in the kind of sauce like this

83
00:03:26.899 --> 00:03:28.746 line:15%
is going to get a little more fussy

84
00:03:28.746 --> 00:03:31.280 line:15%
and it's not necessary, but you can use plum tomatoes.

85
00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.640 line:15%
You can use Marzano tomatoes, fresh, like in-season.

86
00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.600
Just know that it's going to cook down a little longer.

87
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.120
So we have this beautiful sauce over here

88
00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:45.120
and I added some chili flakes, red chili flakes to taste.

89
00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.870
And then basically you have that sauce going

90
00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:51.630
for about 25 minutes, just simmering on low.

91
00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.290
Are we switching, 21, 23.

92
00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:55.440
Voila!

93
00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.213
Ah, beautiful.

94
00:03:58.091 --> 00:04:01.586
So we have this beautiful sauce it's been reducing

95
00:04:01.586 --> 00:04:03.750
and now comes the fun part.

96
00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.453
We're going to toss in our bucatini into the sauce,

97
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.513
right into the sauce.

98
00:04:11.946 --> 00:04:12.953
Right onto my thighs.

99
00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:15.453
That's true.

100
00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.120
Pilar, why the bucatini versus a different type of pasta?

101
00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:19.980
Good question.

102
00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.420
So the sauce is actually from a region specifically

103
00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.720
or a place in Italy where bucatini

104
00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.780
is one of their traditional shapes.

105
00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:32.670
It also has this hole in the middle of it.

106
00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:36.210 line:15%
So it sops up even more sauce, which is really, really nice.

107
00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:39.533 line:15%
But you can use any long shape that you like,

108
00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:41.790 line:15%
spaghetti would work really nicely as well.

109
00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.096
All right.

110
00:04:43.096 --> 00:04:45.760
Last thing we're going to add to this beautiful sauce.

111
00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:46.780
Here we go.

112
00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:51.780 line:15%
We're going to do three tablespoons of parmigiano-reggiano

113
00:04:52.997 --> 00:04:56.883 line:15%
and two tablespoons of pecorino.

114
00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.393 line:15%
And you're going to give that a good mix.

115
00:05:02.554 --> 00:05:03.660 line:15%
Oh, parmigiano and then the pecorino?

116
00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.480
Yeah, so it's two cheeses,

117
00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:08.107
which is really exciting for me.

118
00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:09.560
Are we ready to plate?

119
00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.773
I was just going to say I got the ladle out!

120
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.270
All right, so I am plated up here.

121
00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:18.250
How are you doing, Drew?

122
00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:23.190 line:15%
I am very excited because I think everybody here knows

123
00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:27.675 line:15%
that this show is an entire ruse for me to eat my face off.

124
00:05:27.675 --> 00:05:28.508
(laughing)

125
00:05:28.508 --> 00:05:30.610
And you can finish it with a little more cheese

126
00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:32.300
as you wish.

127
00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.490
I'm going to grate some more just over the top.

128
00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:35.910
All right.

129
00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:37.297
Did we get to try it now?

130
00:05:37.297 --> 00:05:38.224
I sure hope so.

131
00:05:38.224 --> 00:05:39.057
Oh my gosh, you guys.

132
00:05:39.057 --> 00:05:40.331
Again, I feel-

133
00:05:40.331 --> 00:05:41.164
Do we get to try it?

134
00:05:41.164 --> 00:05:44.346
I know, I feel horrible.

135
00:05:44.346 --> 00:05:47.660
You guys are gonna watch us eat this delicious food.

136
00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:48.760
All right, next time.

137
00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:49.980
The surfers cookbook club.

138
00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.320
I'm thinking next time, we'll make sure

139
00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.140
that you have those dishes in front of you

140
00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:57.963
because now I feel guilty.

141
00:05:58.871 --> 00:05:59.704
It looks so good.

142
00:05:59.704 --> 00:06:00.880
All right, ready?

143
00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.439
I'm just going to tell you it's horrible, Daniel,

144
